WEBVTT

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.160
This is Earth, 4.4 billion years ago.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:17.870
A toxic, sterile ball of scorching rubble.

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:20.690
Meteorites rain down on its surface.

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:25.340
Clouds of lethal acid smother it.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:29.090
The Earth seemed destined to remain devoid of life,

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:47.870
But that was about to change.

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:54.800
Another planet, Thea, smashed into Earth,

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.430
and the two planets fused, creating a brand new world,

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:00.000
our world.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:06.050
Even today, Thea lies right beneath our feet.

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:12.320
A smaller chunk of Thea became our moon,

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:22.500
and slowly our oceans formed until life on Earth was ready to begin.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:26.120
But who would inherit this blue planet?

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:35.510
This is the extraordinary story of life before the dinosaurs.

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:45.300
A time when strange and savage creatures fought a ruthless battle to rule the Earth.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:52.610
Amongst them were our own earliest ancestors,

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:57.460
whose survival would decide whether we humans would exist at all.

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:09.910
As they evolved, these bizarre creatures created the blueprint not only for our bodies, but for everything living today.

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:28.420
This is life's forgotten story. Epic war for our world.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.170
A war between monsters.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:10.120
This is our planet, 530 million years ago.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:15.840
Nothing yet lives on land, but in the ocean, it's a different story.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:30.000
Life has already been evolving for millions of years

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:33.580
at a slow and steady pace.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:37.200
The seas are full of simple, soft-bodied creatures

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:41.320
blindly drifting in the currents.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:46.350
Now, however, in the coastal shallows below,

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:49.670
evolution has stepped on the accelerator.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:57.810
Predators have taken their first bite.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.410
This is Anomalocaris, Earth's first super-predator.

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:10.820
This two meter long monster owes his success

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:20.280
to a monumental evolutionary landmark: eyes.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:25.670
They may look bizarre, but they're not unique.

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:29.570
Many predators in the Cambrian seas have also evolved eyes.

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:34.040
and so have their prey.

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:38.200
The consequences have been explosive.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:47.660
Being able to see and react to enemies

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:51.380
has triggered an arms race between hunter and hunted.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:56.210
This battle continues today

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:59.350
and is a major force behind the variety of life.

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:07.470
To combat being visible and vulnerable, 80% of creatures in these shallow seas have sturdy

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:11.750
skeletons on the outside of their bodies.

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:14.770
These armoured animals are called arthropods.

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:20.320
In the future, they'll give rise to insects and spiders.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:24.210
But in these crowded waters, there's competition everywhere.

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:29.090
And even the mighty anomalocaris' defences are constantly put to the test.

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:23.080
The rigid armor splits if bent too far,

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:28.930
leaving the loser vulnerable to a completely different threat.

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:49.470
This is Haiku Ichthes.

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:53.990
He's the size of your thumbnail, but he's an evolutionary giant.

00:06:53.990 --> 00:07:00.000
He's the first ever fish, our earliest known ancestor.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.000
because instead of having armor on the outside, he's tough inside.

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:13.230
He's evolved a primitive backbone.

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:15.640
He's the very first vertebrate,

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:18.640
forerunner of all future backboned animals,

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:23.110
from the dinosaur, to the elephant, to us.

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:29.800
His flexible backbone makes him more maneuverable than spineless anomalocaris,

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:49.260
can scavenge flesh, then dart away unharmed.

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:53.680
Our tiny, backboned ancestors have survived a sea of monsters,

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:56.310
but there are still many more battles ahead.

00:07:56.310 --> 00:08:00.000
They must adapt or die.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:12.600
takes over. As millions of years pass, fish build on their basic design. The

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:18.100
muscles around their backbone evolve into a powerful tail and fins appear.

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:24.770
They evolve a distinct head.

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:38.070
He may not look like you or I, but this odd fish is becoming the blueprint for our own

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:53.210
bodies.

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:54.850
This is Cephalaspis.

00:08:54.850 --> 00:09:00.000
She's a peaceful grazer who sucks up algae through her jawless mouth, but she's also developed

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:05.950
a tough protective head and thick scales.

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:09.730
Our ancestors' arthropod enemies have also been evolving.

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:22.910
And they're ready for round two.

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:26.510
100 million years have passed and the fight for survival

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:29.440
has filled the Silurian seas with variety.

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:41.330
Some creatures here would be recognizable today.

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:49.160
Sponges filter food alongside sea urchins.

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:53.140
The orthocone is a distant relative of squid and cuttlefish,

00:09:53.140 --> 00:09:54.870
but he's as long as a truck.

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:16.000
this world is terrorized by a new improved generation of armored arthropods meet brontoscorpio

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:30.000
his kind may look like modern scorpions but they're a meter long and have gills the arthropods have up their

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:36.430
game. These monsters have a superior armory to Anomalacharis, a stinger the

00:10:36.430 --> 00:10:42.890
size of a light bulb. Scorpions are the top predators in the Silurian seas and

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:55.750
prey is plentiful in their seabed hunting ground. Brontoscorpio zeroes in

00:10:55.750 --> 00:10:57.300
on his next meal.

00:11:12.570 --> 00:11:17.640
But Cephalaspis has evolved an early warning system.

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:21.040
Special sensors on her skin detect the tiniest

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:25.130
vibrations in the water.

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:27.540
We've inherited similar sensors.

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:29.670
They make us sensitive to touch.

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:47.320
With her defensive headgear, Cephalaspis can't swim fast for long.

00:11:47.320 --> 00:11:49.320
She must rest frequently.

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:53.460
Soon, she'll tire completely.

00:12:10.650 --> 00:12:13.230
Cephalaspis suddenly changes her path.

00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:15.630
She's picking up bad vibrations,

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:18.400
something Brontoscorpio can't detect.

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:44.040
Pterygotus is the titan of sea scorpions. The biggest arthropod of all time. More than

00:12:44.040 --> 00:13:00.000
three meters long. She's the size of a crocodile. She's turned the tables on Brontoscorpio.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:18.600
meal for her young. In such dangerous seas there's nowhere to hide. When breeding

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:23.330
season comes, the Cephalaspis congregate to head for the one place they might

00:13:23.330 --> 00:13:30.000
escape the scorpion's grasp: fresh water.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:44.890
in land land at this time is like an alien planet it's a barren expanse of

00:13:44.890 --> 00:13:56.700
roasting rock hotter than the Sahara the air would be toxic to us it has much

00:13:56.700 --> 00:14:00.000
less oxygen and 300 times more carbon dioxide

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:08.570
than today. But some forms of life have gained a foothold in this furnace. The

00:14:08.570 --> 00:14:16.290
first pioneering plants. Cooksonia has a unique survival strategy. It's the first

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:23.300
plant to send shoots upwards, trapping extra light to help it grow. This basic

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:26.660
design will eventually lead to our tallest forests.

00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:41.530
The Cephalaspis convoy ploughs upriver, away from the sea.

00:14:41.530 --> 00:14:47.910
They're making the marathon journey back to the spawning grounds where they hatched.

00:14:47.910 --> 00:14:53.310
Incredibly, our fish ancestors already use memory.

00:14:53.310 --> 00:14:56.120
They use familiar landmarks to navigate.

00:15:00.760 --> 00:15:08.160
Their toughened heads protect a vital weapon, one of the first complex brains.

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:15.230
It's much more developed than their scorpion rivals, who have no memory at all.

00:15:15.230 --> 00:15:22.910
It's thanks to these primitive fish that we can think and solve problems today.

00:15:22.910 --> 00:15:25.600
But the fish have underestimated their enemy.

00:15:33.230 --> 00:15:41.810
It is the arthropods and not our ancestors who have taken the first momentous steps out of the sea on to dry land.

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:47.430
Brontoscorpio has a huge advantage.

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:59.060
As well as gills, he has simple lungs made up of hundreds of thin layers of tissue.

00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:07.880
He can't breathe in and out like we do, he just absorbs the oxygen into his blood.

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:18.850
Equipped to maximize the little oxygen available, and with their armor to protect them from the sun,

00:16:18.850 --> 00:16:25.440
the scorpions patrol the shoreline, scavenging on whatever the sea washes up next.

00:16:38.320 --> 00:16:41.570
Finally, the fish approach their destination.

00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:44.490
They've navigated their way back to the spawning pool

00:16:44.490 --> 00:16:47.350
where their lives began.

00:16:47.350 --> 00:16:49.480
Weak from their long journey,

00:16:49.480 --> 00:16:52.080
now they have to cross a ridge of rock

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:54.390
to make it from the river to the pool.

00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:18.550
The first fish make it through and start to lay their eggs.

00:17:18.550 --> 00:17:23.040
But the exhausted Cephalaspis have company.

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:53.790
Passing scorpions have stumbled on this bounty,

00:17:53.790 --> 00:17:56.720
but the fish have numbers on their side.

00:17:56.720 --> 00:18:00.000
The clever Cephalaspis have navigated their way,

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:06.550
a Scorpio are only here by luck. They're soon stuffed to the gills while the fish

00:18:06.550 --> 00:18:20.110
keep jumping. One scorpion is still hungry but he can't

00:18:20.110 --> 00:18:30.000
feed. He's become a prisoner in his own skin. His rigid skeleton is

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:37.930
now a handicap. It can't grow with his body. He needs to shed his hard skin and

00:18:37.930 --> 00:18:45.990
then grow another, expanding while the new one is still soft. For such a large

00:18:45.990 --> 00:18:48.340
creature this is a long process.

00:19:07.100 --> 00:19:13.060
Next morning, there's no sign of life in the spawning pool.

00:19:13.060 --> 00:19:18.850
The scorpion has missed his chance.

00:19:18.850 --> 00:19:26.030
Our ancestors have survived.

00:19:26.030 --> 00:19:29.250
They've laid their eggs and are returning to the sea.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:36.110
Rain has triumphed over brawn, and soon they won't be such soft targets.

00:19:36.110 --> 00:19:47.830
Evolution starts to give them weapons to fight back.

00:19:47.830 --> 00:19:54.020
Over millions of years, the fish's gills adapt to form the first jaw, with the very first

00:19:54.020 --> 00:19:55.290
teeth.

00:19:55.290 --> 00:19:58.000
Now they're equipped to go on the attack.

00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:04.710
Some develop tougher bones and muscles in their fins and shoulders,

00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:06.950
which become the first limbs.

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:10.850
This is where our arms and legs began.

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:17.510
With this four-limbed design,

00:20:17.540 --> 00:20:22.210
our ancestors finally hauled themselves out of water onto land.

00:20:34.730 --> 00:20:43.510
This is the giant amphibian Hynerpeton, the prototype land dweller for the next 300 million years.

00:21:01.210 --> 00:21:04.070
Hynerpeton are over a metre and a half in length,

00:21:04.070 --> 00:21:06.880
much larger than most amphibians today.

00:21:06.880 --> 00:21:11.750
They've carved out a home along the water's edge.

00:21:11.750 --> 00:21:20.180
Some of their arthropod enemies have now become land specialists,

00:21:20.180 --> 00:21:23.810
but the air here isn't as oxygen-rich as water.

00:21:23.810 --> 00:21:29.860
The arthropod's primitive lungs can only absorb enough oxygen to fuel smaller bodies.

00:21:30.890 --> 00:21:33.930
To adapt, they've had to reduce their size.

00:21:33.930 --> 00:21:36.350
And now their role has been reversed,

00:21:36.350 --> 00:21:42.040
they've helped feed the growth of our amphibian ancestors.

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:55.780
Still, life for this pioneer is far from easy.

00:21:55.780 --> 00:21:57.460
It's a whole new world.

00:22:11.770 --> 00:22:25.250
In the last 50 million years, plants have developed into trees and with nothing around to eat them they've grown into vast forests pumping oxygen into the air.

00:22:32.140 --> 00:22:37.680
Hynerpeton has evolved complex lungs to exploit this new oxygen.

00:22:37.680 --> 00:22:45.140
His lungs are sacs, just like ours, and he breathes like we do,

00:22:45.140 --> 00:22:51.850
forcing air in and out so his blood can absorb more oxygen.

00:22:51.850 --> 00:22:56.080
We still rely on the design developed in this strange amphibian.

00:22:56.080 --> 00:23:00.000
Hynerpeton can breathe online

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:07.730
land, but he's still waterbound. His skin is much thinner than ours and it dries out

00:23:07.730 --> 00:23:17.920
in minutes, so he has to keep it wet. And water is a danger zone.

00:23:30.030 --> 00:23:34.200
The fish are now our ancestors' enemies.

00:23:34.200 --> 00:23:41.270
Primitive sharks are constantly on the hunt.

00:24:01.240 --> 00:24:09.980
But even sharks are small fry, in comparison to some flesh-eating fish.

00:24:09.980 --> 00:24:17.650
Hymeria weighs two tons and is five meters long.

00:24:17.650 --> 00:24:21.600
She's an insatiable carnivore.

00:24:43.440 --> 00:24:52.400
The amphibian's limbs are his saving grace for now.

00:24:52.400 --> 00:25:00.000
As the burning sun dips, Hynerpeton can spend more time on land.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:12.200
of shoreline is his territory and his trump card with the opposite sex.

00:25:12.200 --> 00:25:20.370
Hynerpeton females are choosy and will only go for males who can defend their turf.

00:25:20.370 --> 00:25:23.650
They also only mate during a short season.

00:25:30.640 --> 00:25:34.070
The male's future depends on passing on his genes,

00:25:34.070 --> 00:25:44.940
and tonight could be his last chance.

00:25:44.940 --> 00:25:50.690
As night arrives, so does the competition.

00:25:50.690 --> 00:25:54.100
Another male with his eye on this prime patch.

00:26:10.780 --> 00:26:20.200
To avoid injury, the males demonstrate their strength in a strange push-up contest.

00:26:39.530 --> 00:26:42.140
This rival is not up to the challenge,

00:26:42.140 --> 00:26:45.490
but now our mail may be too late for love.

00:27:01.210 --> 00:27:04.470
And all the females in the area have mated and moved on.

00:27:07.330 --> 00:27:09.650
Hynerpeton seems to have missed his chance.

00:27:10.710 --> 00:27:14.630
The only attention he's attracting comes from the dark waters of the lake.

00:27:21.070 --> 00:27:30.000
The female finally answers his call, and the male seizes the opportunity.

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:53.210
Amphibian eggs are soft, and their young have gills, not lungs,

00:27:53.210 --> 00:27:59.540
so they must be laid in water, where amphibians are most vulnerable.

00:28:15.160 --> 00:28:24.610
Hyneria can attack like a killer whale after a seal, only just missing her prey.

00:28:24.610 --> 00:28:27.620
But she has remarkably powerful fins.

00:28:34.890 --> 00:28:36.890
He takes the mail by surprise.

00:28:48.760 --> 00:28:50.760
The end for this Hynerpeton.

00:28:50.760 --> 00:28:58.980
But the amphibians are about to find a way to leave the dangers of the water behind for good.

00:29:04.350 --> 00:29:10.670
The key to their future success lies in changing their eggs.

00:29:10.670 --> 00:29:13.700
They evolve a hard, waterproof casing,

00:29:13.700 --> 00:29:17.030
which protects the young inside from the drying sun,

00:29:17.030 --> 00:29:21.540
so they can be laid on land.

00:29:21.540 --> 00:29:24.180
The babies will hatch out, fully developed,

00:29:24.180 --> 00:29:29.210
air-breathing, and independent.

00:29:29.210 --> 00:29:30.000
They are the first

00:29:30.030 --> 00:29:45.200
ever true vertebrate land lovers, the very first reptiles, but as they move inland they'll face an

00:29:45.200 --> 00:29:53.360
ancient enemy, more deadly than ever before. The arthropods are back.

00:30:21.020 --> 00:30:27.970
300 million years ago, much of the Earth was covered in vast swamp forests.

00:30:31.470 --> 00:30:39.280
here nothing is as it seems. The 50 meter giants towering above this water world may

00:30:39.280 --> 00:30:51.990
look like trees, but they're actually distant relatives of ferns. Even the air is alien.

00:30:51.990 --> 00:30:58.190
Over a third is oxygen, much higher than at any other time in history. This oxygen-rich

00:30:58.190 --> 00:31:00.000
atmosphere has fueled

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:13.530
the growth of new, super-sized arthropod predators.

00:31:13.530 --> 00:31:17.610
This mesothelae spider is the size of a human head.

00:31:17.610 --> 00:31:23.940
She'd be hunting cats if she were alive today.

00:31:23.940 --> 00:31:28.520
She's an ambush predator, operating from her underground headquarters.

00:31:32.560 --> 00:31:34.560
safe in her burrow, she rests

00:31:34.560 --> 00:31:36.380
her feet on carefully constructed

00:31:36.380 --> 00:31:40.390
trip lines. These allow her

00:31:40.390 --> 00:31:42.250
to pick up vibrations from the

00:31:42.250 --> 00:31:57.960
outside world. Reptile

00:31:57.960 --> 00:32:00.000
Petrolakosaurus is hunting on

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:09.270
the forest floor. Unlike our amphibian ancestors, he has tough, scaly skin, which traps moisture

00:32:09.270 --> 00:32:16.300
inside his body, vital for all land dwellers. Because he doesn't dry out in the sun, he

00:32:16.300 --> 00:32:22.950
can venture away from water. But that means encountering new predators.

00:32:35.820 --> 00:32:51.430
The reptile outruns the spider.

00:32:51.430 --> 00:32:57.400
He's evolved a complex heart, much more efficient than his enemies.

00:32:57.400 --> 00:32:59.320
In fact, the template for our own.

00:33:00.030 --> 00:33:07.580
Powerful pump that pushes blood and oxygen around his body to his muscles so he can run at high speed without stopping for a rest

00:33:07.940 --> 00:33:30.000
This time though he's trapped inside a log first the spider's armor stops her squeezing

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:35.420
But she's not going to give up so easily.

00:33:35.420 --> 00:33:54.780
In her lair, Mesotheli will inject her victim

00:33:54.780 --> 00:33:58.700
with digestive juices to dissolve him from inside.

00:34:04.440 --> 00:34:08.800
Not only the arthropods have flourished in the swamps,

00:34:08.800 --> 00:34:13.330
the Carboniferous is one of Earth's greenest times.

00:34:13.330 --> 00:34:17.980
It's this very abundance of plants

00:34:17.980 --> 00:34:21.580
which has pumped up the air's oxygen to extremes,

00:34:21.580 --> 00:34:24.770
fueling the arthropods' growth spurt.

00:34:32.910 --> 00:34:36.520
The swamp's damp conditions are perfect for plant growth,

00:34:36.520 --> 00:34:40.530
but the water levels are unpredictable.

00:34:40.530 --> 00:34:47.120
They've risen.

00:34:47.120 --> 00:34:50.990
The spider drags her dinner home,

00:34:50.990 --> 00:34:52.470
but something's wrong.

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:07.250
headquarters are flooding. She'll have to evacuate. She can't dig, so she'll have to

00:35:07.250 --> 00:35:22.590
find another hole she can adapt. And once outside, she's vulnerable to larger predators.

00:35:22.590 --> 00:35:27.840
Meganura is a monster dragonfly, queen of the carboniferous skies.

00:35:30.960 --> 00:35:36.160
With a wingspan of almost a meter, she's the size of an eagle with an appetite to match.

00:35:43.460 --> 00:35:49.870
High above her carboniferous kingdom, normally she's invincible, but trouble's brewing.

00:35:51.850 --> 00:35:54.530
The flood below is due to an approaching storm.

00:35:55.850 --> 00:36:00.000
The super oxygenated atmosphere, which has helped fuel the growth of the internet,

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:08.490
insects and arthropods is highly volatile, a lightning strike can trigger an explosion.

00:36:08.490 --> 00:36:21.410
The clock is ticking for the swamp. The rising water levels suit one group of hunters - amphibians.

00:36:21.410 --> 00:36:30.000
They've continued thriving over the last 60 million years. Their thin skin still restrict them to the water's edge, but they're not

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:36.330
powerful predators with a devastating pair of jaws, ready to ambush anything that wanders

00:36:36.330 --> 00:36:50.020
within reach. The homeless spider tries her luck further inland, but other mesotheli fill

00:36:50.020 --> 00:37:00.000
the forest floor and they don't like to share. Neither does

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:08.840
Arthropleura. He's a distant relative of modern millipedes, but as long as a car. He can rear

00:37:08.840 --> 00:37:15.950
up tall enough to look you right in the eye. Although he's vegetarian, his strong jaws could

00:37:15.950 --> 00:37:30.000
still deliver a nasty bite. With the rising waters, Arthropleura

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:34.000
Flourish foraging leads him into dangerous territory.

00:38:00.410 --> 00:38:05.210
In this clash of the titans, the arthropod's armor is his best defense...

00:38:09.280 --> 00:38:14.450
...unless it gets split.

00:38:18.900 --> 00:38:28.260
Impaled, his soft innards provide a feast.

00:38:32.720 --> 00:38:36.760
The lightning storm is building, moving ever closer.

00:38:36.760 --> 00:38:42.200
Just in time, the spider finds another hole.

00:38:42.200 --> 00:38:49.950
A Petrolakosaurus is evicted, but reptiles don't need burrows to avoid danger.

00:38:49.950 --> 00:38:56.200
They have speed and stamina on their side.

00:38:56.200 --> 00:39:00.000
Meanwhile, the spider starts to turn the hole into a hunter's lair.

00:39:12.480 --> 00:39:18.650
Night falls, and Mesothelae settles into her new silk-lined home.

00:39:20.720 --> 00:39:22.340
She tests the new trip lines.

00:39:23.220 --> 00:39:25.760
They're already picking up vibrations from outside.

00:39:27.810 --> 00:39:29.490
The storm is very close now.

00:39:33.040 --> 00:39:38.680
Lightning strikes ignite the oxygen-rich air.

00:39:38.680 --> 00:39:45.770
Giant dragonflies are forced down from the canopy to seek shelter below.

00:39:45.770 --> 00:39:49.430
Unexpected windfall for the water predators.

00:40:03.710 --> 00:40:08.390
Amid the chaos, Misothile seems safe in her bunker underground.

00:40:25.080 --> 00:40:30.000
A huge fire ignited by lightning has devastated Misothile's neighbourhood.

00:40:36.040 --> 00:40:42.970
There are no signs of life above ground.

00:40:42.970 --> 00:40:52.070
However, a petrolakosaurus managed to outrun the flames,

00:40:52.070 --> 00:40:54.870
but now he heads right into the spider's lair.

00:41:09.920 --> 00:41:19.260
Mesa Thilae's hole was at the center of the lightning strike.

00:41:19.260 --> 00:41:25.790
The reptile gets a spider barbecue.

00:41:25.790 --> 00:41:30.000
The era of the giant bugs is coming to an end.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:36.280
drying and the atmosphere is losing oxygen. The monster insects and spiders can't survive

00:41:36.280 --> 00:41:44.600
the change. But with their efficient hearts and waterproof skin, our versatile ancestors

00:41:44.600 --> 00:41:55.140
flourish in the new dry climate. Evolution takes over. Lacking restrictive

00:41:55.140 --> 00:41:59.570
armor means the reptiles can start to make it big.

00:42:03.130 --> 00:42:06.590
Their muscles and bones broaden and extend.

00:42:09.120 --> 00:42:13.570
Their bodies mushroom into huge new shapes and forms.

00:42:15.450 --> 00:42:18.520
Our distant ancestors have come a long way.

00:42:19.110 --> 00:42:21.410
They've now conquered the land.

00:42:31.690 --> 00:42:35.090
The world's first giant reptiles have arrived.

00:42:35.620 --> 00:42:38.060
But they're not related to dinosaurs.

00:42:38.140 --> 00:42:41.880
Their descendants will be of a very different kind.

00:42:48.010 --> 00:42:55.380
Reptiles have now spread all across the globe.

00:43:01.850 --> 00:43:06.710
Hidaphosaurus bask in the early morning sun.

00:43:06.710 --> 00:43:09.830
They're three meters long, as big as hippos,

00:43:09.830 --> 00:43:19.330
and like them, they're vegetarians.

00:43:19.330 --> 00:43:22.770
Life in their new Permian home is a constant battle

00:43:22.770 --> 00:43:25.890
with the elements.

00:43:25.890 --> 00:43:30.000
The climate swings between seasonal extremes, from burning

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:36.740
from summers to freezing winters.

00:43:36.740 --> 00:43:39.020
A new species of plant has evolved,

00:43:39.020 --> 00:43:43.430
able to withstand this cold, dry atmosphere.

00:43:43.430 --> 00:43:47.170
Primitive conifers.

00:43:47.170 --> 00:43:49.620
In this harsh, unpredictable world,

00:43:49.620 --> 00:43:55.710
creatures must be tough and adaptable to survive.

00:43:55.710 --> 00:43:56.990
The insects have shrunk

00:43:56.990 --> 00:43:59.350
since their Carboniferous glory days.

00:44:01.310 --> 00:44:07.360
But the giant reptiles have prospered by finding a way to regulate their body temperature.

00:44:07.360 --> 00:44:17.940
Edaphosaurus are sluggish in the autumn morning chill, but they'll soon warm up thanks to

00:44:17.940 --> 00:44:23.770
the huge sail on their backs.

00:44:23.770 --> 00:44:28.900
Their backbone has tall spines connected by a thin membrane of blood-filled skin.

00:44:30.570 --> 00:44:35.780
This large surface area can lose heat fast if the Adaphosaurus need to cool down.

00:44:38.630 --> 00:44:44.310
While to heat up, they simply turn their sails towards the sun like a solar panel.

00:44:46.470 --> 00:44:55.120
Amazingly, these strange sailbacks are related to us.

00:44:55.420 --> 00:45:00.000
They're part of a very special group of creatures called mammal-like reptiles.

00:45:00.350 --> 00:45:11.840
It's down to them that we have control over our body temperature today.

00:45:11.840 --> 00:45:14.780
But Edaphosaurus aren't the only sailbacks.

00:45:14.780 --> 00:45:18.310
And now their biggest enemy is one of their own kind.

00:45:30.190 --> 00:45:34.110
This is Dimetrodon, a vicious carnivore.

00:45:34.110 --> 00:45:39.330
The biggest reptile on Earth.

00:45:39.330 --> 00:45:43.490
She'd normally attack adult Edaphosaurus,

00:45:43.490 --> 00:45:45.840
but today she wants to avoid injury.

00:45:45.840 --> 00:45:49.140
She's pregnant and almost ready to lay her eggs.

00:45:52.780 --> 00:45:55.640
She finds a smaller, softer target.

00:46:19.100 --> 00:46:20.980
This was only a mock charge,

00:46:20.980 --> 00:46:22.860
a tactic to disperse the group

00:46:22.860 --> 00:46:26.420
and expose the vulnerable young.

00:46:26.420 --> 00:46:29.640
The panicking Edaphosaurus flushed their sails with blood

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:36.080
lashing an eye spot to distract the predator's attention from their vulnerable head.

00:46:36.080 --> 00:46:39.190
The Dimetrodon can see through their disguise.

00:47:00.030 --> 00:47:29.640
on to her victim. As well as her sheer size and power, Dimetrodon has a killer edge.

00:47:31.530 --> 00:47:35.900
As a mammal-like reptile, she's evolved specialized teeth.

00:47:35.900 --> 00:47:39.360
Incisors shear off chunks of flesh.

00:47:39.360 --> 00:47:42.290
Serrated canines cut and slice.

00:47:42.290 --> 00:47:46.980
We humans have inherited these same meat-eating teeth.

00:47:46.980 --> 00:47:53.180
We have a smaller version in our mouths today.

00:47:53.180 --> 00:47:58.340
The young Edaphosaurus didn't stand a chance.

00:47:58.340 --> 00:48:00.000
This is a crucial kill for the dimension.

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:04.950
Before she lays she needs to eat enough to last her for the next seven months.

00:48:04.950 --> 00:48:11.280
To boost her baby's chances of survival, she's evolved a basic form of parental care,

00:48:11.280 --> 00:48:13.950
guarding her eggs.

00:48:13.950 --> 00:48:20.480
But the smell of blood has attracted more Dimetrodon, highly aggressive males.

00:48:34.730 --> 00:48:38.290
The female knows better than to defend her kill.

00:48:40.630 --> 00:48:44.760
By the time the group has finished, there'll be nothing left but bones.

00:48:48.590 --> 00:48:55.030
While lions today consume 70% of a carcass, dimetrodon eat 90.

00:48:57.780 --> 00:49:00.000
But they'll only eat intestines after shakes.

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:02.870
and taking out the waste inside.

00:49:02.870 --> 00:49:22.340
Dung is one thing a Dimetrodon can't stomach.

00:49:22.340 --> 00:49:25.990
The female has built a nesting mound of earth and wood.

00:49:35.820 --> 00:49:49.240
Inside, she digs a deep burrow and starts to lay her eggs.

00:50:01.880 --> 00:50:08.750
She covers her brood carefully, ready to begin her guard duty.

00:50:08.750 --> 00:50:16.290
Egg thieves lurk in the shadows, like this carnivorous amphibian.

00:50:16.290 --> 00:50:19.910
But he's too small to make a full frontal attack.

00:50:19.910 --> 00:50:24.430
He'll have to bide his time until the female turns her back.

00:50:24.430 --> 00:50:30.000
The mother must also constantly monitor the nest's temperature.

00:50:32.970 --> 00:50:36.530
Temperature controls vital for reptiles even before they hatch.

00:50:36.530 --> 00:50:41.750
The mother piles on more earth if the babies are too cold

00:50:41.750 --> 00:50:46.010
and will remove a layer if they're too warm.

00:50:46.010 --> 00:50:48.020
Otherwise, the babies will die.

00:50:48.020 --> 00:50:56.980
It's going to be a long seven months.

00:51:12.120 --> 00:51:28.090
Months pass, autumn becomes a bitterly cold winter.

00:51:28.090 --> 00:51:30.000
The landscape is transformed.

00:51:36.010 --> 00:51:42.480
An eerie silence descends as all creatures seek shelter.

00:51:42.480 --> 00:51:53.740
Still the female keeps her vigil, getting weaker all the time from lack of food.

00:51:53.740 --> 00:51:56.450
This is the most critical time for her young.

00:51:56.450 --> 00:52:00.000
The nest temperature can plummet to dangerous levels.

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:12.420
of the young may never hatch out.

00:52:12.420 --> 00:52:27.390
Then finally, winter melts into spring.

00:52:27.390 --> 00:52:30.000
In the morning's warmth, the Adaphosaurus

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:36.910
on the thriving conifers.

00:52:36.910 --> 00:52:40.090
These trees contain an early form of antifreeze,

00:52:40.090 --> 00:52:44.210
which has helped them to withstand the cold winter.

00:52:44.210 --> 00:52:47.330
They also have extremely tough, hardy leaves,

00:52:47.330 --> 00:52:52.840
making them particularly difficult to digest.

00:52:52.840 --> 00:52:55.260
To grind down these tough mouthfuls,

00:52:55.260 --> 00:52:56.760
the Adaphosaurus have developed

00:52:56.760 --> 00:53:00.000
a specialized jaw and teeth.

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:03.080
first creatures with this adaptation.

00:53:03.080 --> 00:53:06.290
Digesting plants is a more challenging task than meat,

00:53:06.290 --> 00:53:08.490
and the Adaphosaurus' vast stomach

00:53:08.490 --> 00:53:15.120
acts as a non-stop processing factory.

00:53:15.120 --> 00:53:17.560
When the hot midday sun rises,

00:53:17.560 --> 00:53:30.000
the herd laces beneath the conifers to digest.

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:42.160
shade their sails from the heat but there's much activity at the nesting

00:53:42.160 --> 00:53:50.350
mound and it's not the mother who's digging another female is desperate to

00:53:50.350 --> 00:54:00.000
lay her eggs if she takes over the mound she'll dig out and kill the incubator

00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:14.120
The resident mother may be weak, but she won't give up her nest without a fight.

00:54:32.330 --> 00:54:35.350
It's a battle neither can afford to lose.

00:55:02.620 --> 00:55:13.300
Next morning, just one female remains at the nesting mound.

00:55:13.300 --> 00:55:15.490
It's the resident mother.

00:55:15.490 --> 00:55:22.550
She's managed to wrestle off her rival, but not without a sacrifice.

00:55:22.550 --> 00:55:30.000
She won't be able to defend her eggs or herself for much longer.

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:43.720
The thieving amphibian is poised to seize his chance.

00:55:43.720 --> 00:55:52.740
As summer arrives, the Adaphosaurus are thriving in the heat.

00:55:52.740 --> 00:55:56.690
The young have grown and test out their newfound strength.

00:56:06.240 --> 00:56:09.540
But the mother Dimetrodon has reached the end of hers.

00:56:09.540 --> 00:56:15.620
So weak, she's helpless as another intruder attacks her nest.

00:56:15.620 --> 00:56:26.150
Luckily, this large male has got his teeth into something more substantial than her young:

00:56:26.150 --> 00:56:28.390
the amphibian.

00:56:33.640 --> 00:56:42.180
the precious eggs are safe and ready to hatch the mother can hear them squeaking she's achieved her

00:56:42.180 --> 00:56:50.110
goal but at this moment her maternal bond is severed unlike her mammal descendants

00:56:50.110 --> 00:56:56.500
she won't nurture her young her instinct now is to save herself by finding food

00:57:20.320 --> 00:57:21.400
their mother's protection.

00:57:22.150 --> 00:57:24.410
The babies will have to be ready to go it alone.

00:57:27.660 --> 00:57:30.000
They take their first steps into a parallel

00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:43.020
world and must make a mad dash for the safety of the trees danger in a familiar

00:57:43.020 --> 00:57:50.160
form awaits them adult Dimetrodon are cannibals

00:58:09.690 --> 00:58:11.680
Not all babies are helpless.

00:58:11.680 --> 00:58:15.610
Some already instinctively know how to defend themselves.

00:58:15.610 --> 00:58:22.840
They roll in dung, something the adults can't stand,

00:58:22.840 --> 00:58:27.620
repelling their pursuers long enough to get a head start.

00:58:49.230 --> 00:58:55.550
One adult is driven on by hunger and desperation, the mother.

00:58:55.550 --> 00:59:00.000
She'll weed out weaker babies straight away, which helps the strong ones and her

00:59:00.000 --> 00:59:13.000
species as a whole, survive. And now the reptiles evolve to tighten their grip on land. Their

00:59:13.000 --> 00:59:20.440
legs straighten and lengthen, holding them more upright and giving them speed. Their

00:59:20.440 --> 00:59:30.000
backbones and muscles get stronger, and they even start to look like mammals, to house a larger

00:59:30.000 --> 00:59:35.520
brain their skull bones expand into an enormous head and out of their jaws

00:59:35.520 --> 00:59:50.920
thrust human sharp teeth a new age of specialist reptiles has dawned but

00:59:50.920 --> 00:59:56.120
after 270 million years of evolution the war our distant ancestors have

00:59:56.120 --> 01:00:00.000
waged so long is much more than a battle between predator and

01:00:00.190 --> 01:00:10.180
prey, the whole planet is entering a different kind of crisis, one that will decimate the

01:00:10.180 --> 01:00:17.730
monster reptiles and wipe out 90% of life on Earth.

01:00:31.690 --> 01:00:39.530
All the continents on Earth have now drifted together to create one giant landmass called Pangea.

01:00:39.530 --> 01:00:44.530
And at its center lies the largest desert the world has ever seen.

01:00:44.530 --> 01:00:55.380
It's a brutal world where animals have to be especially tough, perfect for reptiles.

01:00:55.380 --> 01:01:00.000
This is a one-ton Siberian Scutosaurus, a distant animal.

01:01:00.000 --> 01:01:07.160
Ancestor of turtles although he has no shell his back is covered in hard bony plates and just as well

01:01:07.440 --> 01:01:11.100
These sand dunes hide a fearsome predator

01:01:12.880 --> 01:01:14.880
Scootasaurus normally travel in herds

01:01:15.300 --> 01:01:20.390
But this old males got left behind and his keen nose senses danger

01:01:31.630 --> 01:02:00.000
once he tires he's vulnerable his attacker

01:02:00.000 --> 01:02:02.040
is a carnivorous Gorgonopsid.

01:02:06.380 --> 01:02:12.520
She's fast, powerful, and equipped with deadly weapons

01:02:12.520 --> 01:02:14.180
no hunter has had before.

01:02:17.950 --> 01:02:25.980
Exhausted, trapped, and weakened by his wound,

01:02:25.980 --> 01:02:28.420
the old Scootosaurus has no fight left.

01:02:30.320 --> 01:02:33.520
All he can do is wait for the killer blow.

01:02:43.950 --> 01:02:59.640
The Gorgonopsid's huge jaws hold a pair of fangs 12 centimeters long.

01:03:00.030 --> 01:03:03.280
She has evolved the world's first sabre teeth,

01:03:03.280 --> 01:03:07.200
a lethal weapon some mammal predators will later use.

01:03:16.690 --> 01:03:20.770
In this harsh landscape,

01:03:20.770 --> 01:03:24.280
the reptile's biggest battle is now with the elements.

01:03:24.280 --> 01:03:27.340
A massive surge in volcanic activity

01:03:27.340 --> 01:03:30.000
is beginning to super-heat the atmosphere.

01:03:30.380 --> 01:03:37.610
creating the highest temperatures life on earth has ever known.

01:03:37.610 --> 01:03:39.830
Each year the deserts spread.

01:03:39.830 --> 01:03:43.510
Even here in Siberia, far north of the equator,

01:03:43.510 --> 01:03:45.450
animals are forced to cluster around

01:03:45.450 --> 01:03:56.230
any lasting source of water, no matter how small.

01:03:56.230 --> 01:04:00.000
The huge female gorgonopsid has come looking for a drink

01:04:00.030 --> 01:04:07.070
wash down the Scootosaurus, and even smaller Gorgonopsits will do well to keep out of her way.

01:04:07.070 --> 01:04:12.060
Five meters long from nose to tail, she's the top predator on Earth.

01:04:12.060 --> 01:04:20.160
There'll be nothing to rival her until the dinosaurs evolve in 30 million years' time.

01:04:20.160 --> 01:04:27.510
But unlike the dinosaurs, these early reptiles are more closely linked to our ancestors, mammals,

01:04:27.510 --> 01:04:30.000
than to lizards and crocodiles.

01:04:30.440 --> 01:04:35.220
They don't have fur, but they've already evolved scent glands in their skin.

01:04:35.220 --> 01:04:43.770
This way everyone can tell when the giant gorgonopsid is around.

01:04:43.770 --> 01:04:54.570
Despite the presence of these heavyweights, smaller creatures also eke out a living around the waterhole.

01:04:54.570 --> 01:04:59.340
Diictodon, a hardy little burrower, just half a meter long.

01:05:02.970 --> 01:05:08.070
They live as pairs in spiral burrows, which remain cool even in the desert heat.

01:05:08.750 --> 01:05:13.810
Like the large predators outside, diictodon are distant reptile relatives of mammals.

01:05:14.390 --> 01:05:18.250
And although it will be 30 million years before the first true mammals appear,

01:05:18.250 --> 01:05:20.870
there are already family resemblances.

01:05:22.620 --> 01:05:26.300
In particular, his hearing is linked to tiny bones in his lower jaw.

01:05:27.020 --> 01:05:30.000
One day, these will evolve into our middle ear bones.

01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:32.200
something no reptile has.

01:05:32.200 --> 01:05:41.250
Several pairs of diictodon live around the waterhole,

01:05:41.250 --> 01:05:42.770
but this is not a colony.

01:05:42.770 --> 01:05:45.660
They compete furiously for the sparse vegetation

01:05:45.660 --> 01:05:47.020
that is their food.

01:05:47.020 --> 01:05:49.870
This frequently leads to confrontation.

01:05:52.080 --> 01:06:00.000
These clashes are one reason why both men

01:06:00.000 --> 01:06:16.910
and females sport a pair of short tusks there is however one big advantage to

01:06:16.910 --> 01:06:22.830
living in a crowd if a neighbor spots danger everyone soon knows about it a

01:06:22.830 --> 01:06:29.960
gorgonopsid has come to investigate

01:06:40.300 --> 01:06:49.560
A single snap of those enormous jaws would crush Diictodon, if they could get a grip.

01:06:49.560 --> 01:06:54.070
But it's not always size that counts.

01:07:01.180 --> 01:07:05.340
These tiny mouthfuls are more trouble than they're worth.

01:07:05.340 --> 01:07:10.410
The Gorgonopsid knows that if she hangs around the waterhole for long enough,

01:07:10.410 --> 01:07:14.890
eventually larger prey will come to her.

01:07:14.890 --> 01:07:30.000
There is another unseen resident of this waterhole.

01:07:32.460 --> 01:07:38.960
A monster much less suited than the reptiles to the changing climate, a fugitive from when

01:07:38.960 --> 01:07:43.320
this desert world was lush and green.

01:07:43.320 --> 01:07:58.780
It lies in ambush under water like a crocodile, a giant amphibian labyrinthodont, but pound

01:07:58.780 --> 01:08:00.000
for pound this female

01:08:00.000 --> 01:08:08.020
males picked the wrong target.

01:08:08.020 --> 01:08:12.970
The amphibian's ambush attempt was probably driven by sheer desperation.

01:08:12.970 --> 01:08:17.910
For generations, this waterhole has supported a wide range of animals, including several

01:08:17.910 --> 01:08:20.010
other labyrinthodonts.

01:08:20.010 --> 01:08:25.600
But this one is the last, and as the water she depends on disappears, she's trapped.

01:08:37.550 --> 01:08:41.220
The wet season arrives, but there's no rain.

01:08:41.220 --> 01:08:42.920
With life under such pressure,

01:08:42.920 --> 01:08:45.840
species are dying out at a rate that won't be matched

01:08:45.840 --> 01:08:51.310
until humans evolve in 250 million years' time.

01:08:51.310 --> 01:08:55.000
The waterhole is now no more than a large puddle.

01:09:00.090 --> 01:09:04.800
Directed on a more adaptable than most they can dig deeper to escape the heat

01:09:05.240 --> 01:09:07.300
carving out tunnels in the rock-hard mud

01:09:11.280 --> 01:09:19.840
But the giant amphibian is in big trouble and as she grows weaker her enemy watches and waits

01:09:20.400 --> 01:09:23.780
It's a deadly game of cat and mouse

01:09:31.400 --> 01:09:34.480
But life here is about to change for everyone.

01:09:38.720 --> 01:09:59.640
A grating herd of Scootasaurus has sniffed out the waterhole.

01:10:08.960 --> 01:10:15.300
possible they smelt the water several miles away and this could well be the first drink they've had in six months

01:10:16.100 --> 01:10:30.000
Desert plants are a poor source of nutrients, but these large reptiles are designed to squeeze out every ounce of

01:10:30.320 --> 01:10:36.290
Although they cannot chew, their huge stomachs hold stones they've picked up

01:10:36.290 --> 01:10:41.680
from the desert. These help grind the vegetation so their digestive juices can

01:10:41.680 --> 01:11:00.000
get to work. The tough little diictodon are used to dodging danger, but the gorgonopsid now

01:11:00.000 --> 01:11:24.180
keeps her distance. Even she dare not attack such a large armored herd. It's a standoff.

01:11:24.180 --> 01:11:30.000
It turns out the Scootasaurus are a bigger threat to life around the waterhole than the

01:11:30.510 --> 01:11:32.880
Within a few days, they have drunk it dry

01:11:32.880 --> 01:11:42.370
and moved on once again in their eternal search for food.

01:11:42.370 --> 01:11:54.780
They represent the plight of all creatures on the continent of Pangaea.

01:11:54.780 --> 01:11:59.830
The global drought is now obliterating millions of years of evolution.

01:12:00.570 --> 01:12:17.610
Life everywhere is on the brink.

01:12:17.610 --> 01:12:30.000
Animals that do not migrate, the situation's reaching a crisis point.

01:12:30.000 --> 01:12:33.360
She drives the Gorgonopsid back to the diictodon,

01:12:33.360 --> 01:12:45.610
and this time she tries to dig them out.

01:12:45.610 --> 01:12:48.620
But the diictodon's hard work pays off.

01:12:48.620 --> 01:12:55.940
Their tunnel in the sun-baked earth is deep enough to keep them safe.

01:12:55.940 --> 01:13:00.000
Instead, the Gorgonopsid's nose leads her back to the dried-up waterhole.

01:13:00.830 --> 01:13:10.810
She senses things are not as lifeless as they seem.

01:13:10.810 --> 01:13:22.130
Giant amphibian.

01:13:22.130 --> 01:13:24.620
Just before the waterhole dried up,

01:13:24.620 --> 01:13:27.070
she must have burrowed into the damp mud,

01:13:27.070 --> 01:13:30.000
forming a cocoon around herself.

01:13:30.000 --> 01:13:35.370
last-ditch attempt to sit out the drought.

01:13:35.370 --> 01:13:38.330
But in her torpid state, she's helpless

01:13:38.330 --> 01:13:41.490
as the Gorgonopsid feasts.

01:14:02.040 --> 01:14:09.000
Eventually, storm clouds gather, but instead of rain water, they bring sand.

01:14:09.000 --> 01:14:22.370
Sandstorm sweeps in, burying the waterhole completely.

01:14:46.350 --> 01:14:49.680
The vast Pangean deserts continue to spread,

01:14:49.680 --> 01:14:52.810
snuffing out entire ecosystems.

01:14:52.810 --> 01:14:56.480
Even the mighty Gorgonopsid has at last succumbed.

01:14:56.480 --> 01:14:59.860
With no prey, even she could not survive.

01:15:00.700 --> 01:15:10.040
Mummified in the sand, this monster will soon be nothing but dust.

01:15:10.040 --> 01:15:14.910
Over the next few million years, the Earth will suffocate under a stagnant blanket of

01:15:14.910 --> 01:15:22.140
heat, sparking a mass extinction even greater than the one that will destroy the dinosaurs.

01:15:22.140 --> 01:15:30.000
The future belongs to a tiny group of survivors, small and adaptable enough to sit it out.

01:15:30.000 --> 01:15:32.820
carry on digging throughout the drought.

01:15:32.820 --> 01:15:35.410
This pair has unearthed plant tubers,

01:15:35.410 --> 01:15:37.770
which hold enough water and nutrients

01:15:37.770 --> 01:15:48.390
to keep them going for several months.

01:15:48.390 --> 01:15:52.750
As millions of years go by, the climate shifts again,

01:15:52.750 --> 01:15:54.630
and the reptiles are among the first

01:15:54.630 --> 01:15:58.300
to recover and repopulate the empty earth.

01:15:58.300 --> 01:16:00.000
From creatures like little diictodons

01:16:00.540 --> 01:16:08.230
larger, stronger herbivores evolve. These tough forerunners of mammals seem poised to

01:16:08.230 --> 01:16:30.000
seize control for good, but they are in fact set to play out their final scene. The dawn

01:16:30.000 --> 01:16:36.200
Triassic era and the earth has only just begun to show signs of recovery. The

01:16:36.200 --> 01:16:41.160
deserts have stopped growing and huge forests of primitive conifer start to

01:16:41.160 --> 01:16:51.600
recolonize the land. In these new forests plant eaters recover first, one in

01:16:51.600 --> 01:16:57.010
particular. Like most of the large reptiles they may resemble dinosaurs but

01:16:57.010 --> 01:17:00.000
Lystrosaurus are more closely linked to mammals.

01:17:00.440 --> 01:17:07.050
And to us, astonishingly, their vast herds make up more than half of all life on Earth.

01:17:07.860 --> 01:17:11.270
Never again will a single species do so well.

01:17:16.210 --> 01:17:19.590
Deep within the forests, though, a rival is evolving.

01:17:20.200 --> 01:17:24.880
A new type of animal destined to change the face of life on Earth.

01:17:31.630 --> 01:17:35.810
Meet Euparkeria, a tiny insect eater.

01:17:35.810 --> 01:17:38.950
He may not look much of a threat to the dominant reptiles,

01:17:38.950 --> 01:17:43.680
but the key to his success is in his hip.

01:17:43.680 --> 01:17:46.190
The way his thigh bone is attached

01:17:46.190 --> 01:17:57.000
allows him to run on two legs, freeing his hands.

01:17:57.000 --> 01:18:00.000
This kind of agility has never been seen before

01:18:00.000 --> 01:18:19.090
reptiles and gives Euparkaria an edge. Euparkaria will lay the foundations for a

01:18:19.090 --> 01:18:27.910
new group of reptiles: the dinosaurs. Giants such as Tyrannosaurus and

01:18:27.910 --> 01:18:30.000
Diplodocus can all trace their

01:18:30.000 --> 01:18:44.500
family tree back to this little insect hunter in the Triassic forests.

01:18:44.500 --> 01:18:51.000
The monster herds of Lystrosaurus hoover up so much vegetation that they constantly migrate

01:18:51.000 --> 01:18:53.270
to find fresh food.

01:18:53.270 --> 01:18:58.760
They follow the seasonal rains, and each year in Antarctica, this brings them to the edge

01:18:58.760 --> 01:19:00.000
of a huge escrow.

01:19:04.320 --> 01:19:09.190
The only way down for these cumbersome animals is through a deep ravine.

01:19:32.080 --> 01:19:37.780
The herd moves mostly at night, and come sunset, they begin the dangerous descent.

01:19:39.720 --> 01:19:43.440
It will take several hours for all of them to pass through the ravine,

01:19:44.020 --> 01:19:49.870
and in some parts there's only space to move in single file, which leaves them very vulnerable.

01:20:02.520 --> 01:20:10.360
Lystrosaurus have sharp eyesight and a very good sense of smell, but in these cramped

01:20:10.360 --> 01:20:13.670
conditions it's hard to spot trouble ahead.

01:20:13.670 --> 01:20:30.000
They're being watched by a vicious nocturnal hunter, a theracephalian.

01:20:30.000 --> 01:20:32.090
and Lystrosaurus are strong opponents,

01:20:32.090 --> 01:20:45.510
but this hunter doesn't rely on power to make a kill.

01:20:45.510 --> 01:20:49.970
The attack is swift and seems to have been unsuccessful,

01:20:49.970 --> 01:20:53.670
but this predator's secret weapon is a poisonous bite.

01:20:53.670 --> 01:20:56.500
Its venom is more lethal than a black mambas,

01:20:56.500 --> 01:20:59.380
and it quickly floods the Lystrosaurus's bloodstream,

01:21:00.960 --> 01:21:05.350
All the theracephalians have to do is wait for it to take effect.

01:21:09.540 --> 01:21:13.320
The victim collapses, but the herd moves on without concern.

01:21:13.420 --> 01:21:15.400
They have no social bonds.

01:21:25.670 --> 01:21:28.550
The theracephalians close in for the kill.

01:21:42.030 --> 01:21:46.560
dawn at the foot of the escarpment and here the Lystrosaurus will face their

01:21:46.560 --> 01:21:51.920
biggest challenge yet. They need to cross a river but its banks are lined with

01:21:51.920 --> 01:22:00.000
open jaws. Chasmatosaurs are the earliest ancestors of crocodiles and

01:22:00.000 --> 01:22:05.160
alligators. Their strange overbite means once they get their teeth into a victim, there

01:22:05.160 --> 01:22:10.530
is no chance of escape. They don't normally gather in such numbers, but they're here for

01:22:10.530 --> 01:22:19.390
a special event: the annual migration of the Lystrosaurus. If you're a Euparkaria, the

01:22:19.390 --> 01:22:24.090
river's not a great place for hunting insects, but he has speed on his side.

01:22:36.460 --> 01:22:41.230
As the sun rises, the chasmatosaurs are drawn towards the water.

01:22:41.230 --> 01:22:46.140
Some of them haven't eaten since the Lystrosaurus last passed this way a year ago.

01:22:46.140 --> 01:22:48.760
Now they sense it's time to feed again.

01:23:00.670 --> 01:23:05.230
The front of the vast Lystrosaurus herd finally reaches the river.

01:23:05.230 --> 01:23:21.550
They're strong swimmers, but drowning is not the danger here.

01:23:21.550 --> 01:23:25.880
They linger at the river's edge, reluctant to go in.

01:23:25.880 --> 01:23:30.000
For reptiles, they have relatively large brains, and they may remember what

01:23:30.000 --> 01:23:35.600
happened last year. But with more Lystrosaurus arriving all the time, the

01:23:35.600 --> 01:23:49.930
backlog will soon force them in. On the other side lush grazing beckons and

01:23:49.930 --> 01:23:53.080
eventually the first one takes the plunge.

01:24:02.520 --> 01:24:05.830
A strong swimmer, he quickly makes it to the other side.

01:24:05.830 --> 01:24:09.840
But with the steep banks, he's still got to find a place where he can get ashore.

01:24:09.840 --> 01:24:13.480
And every extra moment in the water increases the risk.

01:24:13.480 --> 01:24:27.680
More and more Lystrosaurus warily strike out for the other side.

01:24:32.490 --> 01:24:50.060
But now the predators are homing in.

01:24:50.060 --> 01:24:57.360
First swimmer has made it and clambers ashore

01:24:57.360 --> 01:24:59.960
that many of the herd now entering the water

01:25:00.000 --> 01:25:02.760
will not be so lucky.

01:26:02.330 --> 01:26:14.590
Despite the carnage, the herd is soon reforming on the other side.

01:26:14.590 --> 01:26:18.300
Predators are quickly sated and exhausted.

01:26:18.300 --> 01:26:23.680
Although many Lystrosaurus are dead, it's still only a small proportion of the herd.

01:26:31.600 --> 01:26:34.360
But the brief glory days of these strange,

01:26:34.360 --> 01:26:37.040
mammal-like reptiles will soon be gone.

01:26:44.810 --> 01:26:51.920
As life continues to revive after the Permian extinction,

01:26:51.920 --> 01:26:56.000
the battle for survival intensifies.

01:26:56.000 --> 01:26:59.350
You park area may only have had a walk-on part so far,

01:27:00.000 --> 01:27:15.130
he's about to herald a new age of lethal predators.

01:27:15.130 --> 01:27:19.470
Descendants will be known as the most notorious monsters

01:27:19.470 --> 01:27:28.520
of them all, dinosaurs.

01:27:37.070 --> 01:27:46.280
The unique ability to stand upright and the agility this allowed gives the early dinosaurs a competitive edge over our four-legged mammal-like ancestors.

01:27:48.110 --> 01:27:54.190
Repressed by these swift killers, our ancestors are reduced to small creatures hiding in the shadows.

01:27:56.200 --> 01:27:57.520
Their time will come...

01:28:00.060 --> 01:28:02.620
for the next 170 million years,

01:28:02.620 --> 01:28:05.830
the world belongs to the dinosaurs.
